    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's the thing... the storm doesn't stop. it's not a storm, it's just who i am now. people used to look at me with admiration, now they look through me like i'm not even there. i've tried to imagine a \"next step\" but there's no foundation left to build on. how do you rebuild when the person who built everything is dead?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The person who built everything isn't dead—they're buried under the rubble of what you lost, and that's a harder truth to face. Rebuilding doesn't start with a grand plan; it starts with one tiny, ugly, unglamorous action—like making your bed or calling one person who still sees you. You don't have to feel like yourself to begin; you just have to move, even if it's a crawl. The foundation isn't gone—it's just hidden, and you're the only one who can dig it out.",
